# WHY overlay: replaces a question's key idea with a longer 3-5 sentence rationale (mechanism first).
# Each key is the question text or a substring of it that matches exactly one question.
WHY = {
 "daughter of a client with advanced cancer": "Hospice is a form of care that aims at comfort, so everything it does is palliative. Palliative care is the wider umbrella: it treats symptoms such as pain, dyspnea and nausea at any stage and can run alongside chemotherapy, radiation or surgery. Because palliative care does not require the client to stop curative treatment, it is not always hospice. This is why “all hospice is palliative, but not all palliative care is hospice” is accurate. (study guide, Other Topics; Ch 26)",
 "A client in hospice care has dyspnea and pain": "At the end of life the goal changes from cure to comfort, so symptoms are treated on a schedule rather than waiting for them to become severe. Opioids such as morphine relieve pain and the sensation of breathlessness, and they are given as ordered rather than withheld. Mouth care and repositioning prevent the discomfort that comes with reduced intake and immobility. The nurse also honors the advance directive and DNR order and supports the family. (study guide, Other Topics; standard content)",
 "A client has a 2 cm breast mass and the plan is chemotherapy before surgery": "The timing words describe when a treatment is given relative to the definitive treatment, which here is the mastectomy. Neoadjuvant treatment comes before it and shrinks the tumor, which is why the chemotherapy in this plan is neoadjuvant. Adjuvant treatment comes after it and targets microscopic cells that may be left behind. Radiation and oral hormonal therapy after surgery are therefore adjuvant. (study guide, Ch 13 case and class PLQ)",
 "Place these platelet counts in order": "Platelets plug injured vessels, so the lower the count the less able the body is to stop bleeding. At 150,000 the count is at the lower limit of normal, and below it the client has thrombocytopenia. Precautions begin below 100,000, spontaneous bleeding becomes possible below 50,000, and platelets are usually transfused below 20,000 or before procedures. Below 5,000 a fatal central nervous system or GI bleed is possible. (study guide, Ch 33, Ch 34 and cheat sheet)",
 "reticulocytes of 4%": "Reticulocytes are immature red cells released from the marrow, and they are larger than mature red cells. After a hemorrhage the marrow speeds up to replace lost cells, so more immature cells appear in the blood and the reticulocyte count rises above the normal 0.5 to 2.5%. In a marrow that is not producing, such as aplastic or pernicious anemia, the count is low instead. The count therefore tells the nurse how well the marrow is responding. (study guide, Ch 33)",
 "A client has iron deficiency anemia. Which laboratory findings": "Iron is stored as ferritin and carried by transferrin, so when iron runs out the stores empty and ferritin, serum iron and transferrin saturation all fall. The transferrin carriers are left with many empty binding spots, which is why total iron-binding capacity rises. Without enough iron the marrow makes small, pale red cells, so the MCV and MCHC fall. The class slide gives ferritin below 100 for iron deficiency and the textbook gives below 10; ask your instructor which number will be tested. (study guide, Ch 33 and Ch 34)",
 "preparing to collect blood for a CBC": "The tube additive decides which test can be run. A CBC needs whole blood that does not clot, so it goes in a lavender tube with EDTA. Clotting studies measure how fast plasma clots, so they use sodium citrate, which is reversible, and the tube must be filled completely to keep the blood-to-additive ratio right. The tourniquet comes off as soon as blood flows because leaving it on for more than a minute falsely raises the RBC, Hgb and Hct. (study guide, Ch 33)",
 "typically show microcytic and hypochromic red cells": "Hemoglobin needs iron, and the amount of hemoglobin determines how large and how dark a red cell is. When iron is lacking the marrow makes cells with less hemoglobin, so they are smaller (a low MCV, microcytic) and paler (a low MCHC, hypochromic). Lead poisoning produces the same picture. Large cells, by contrast, point to a lack of B12 or folate. (study guide, Ch 33)",
 "smoked two packs a day for decades": "Smoking causes mutations that turn off the controls on cell growth, and the two lung cancer groups behave very differently once that happens. Small cell cancer is about 15% of cases, grows fast and spreads to other organs early, so removing the tumor does not cure it and surgery is not the main treatment. Its outlook is poor at any stage: only 5 to 10% survive 5 years, and your class notes give a life expectancy of 6 months to 1 year. Non-small cell cancer is about 85%, has three cell types, grows more slowly, and has a good 5-year survival if found early, so surgery is used when there is no metastasis. (study guide, Ch 26 and class notes)",
 "scheduled for a PET scan": "A PET scan detects how metabolically active tissue is, and rapidly dividing cancer cells are very active, so they light up as hot spots (glucose must be controlled before the scan). That is why PET reveals metabolism rather than anatomy. Structure and tissue are shown by other tests: chest x-ray and CT find the lesion, sputum cytology may identify tumor cells, bronchoscopy allows a biopsy of the airways, and mediastinoscopy is a surgical incision to view and biopsy the mediastinum. (study guide, Ch 26)",
 "WHO pain ladder used for cancer pain": "The ladder matches the strength of the drug to the intensity of the pain. A nonopioid is first, and it is kept at every step because it adds pain relief and lets the opioid dose stay lower. When pain is moderate a weaker opioid is added, and when it is severe a strong opioid such as morphine or fentanyl is used. Doses are given around the clock rather than on demand, so that the level of relief stays steady and pain peaks are avoided. (study guide, Ch 26 and Ch 11)",
 "continuous bubbling in the water-seal": "The water-seal chamber works as a one-way valve: air leaves the pleural space and bubbles through the water, but cannot come back. A brief bubble when the client coughs is expected, but bubbling that never stops means air is entering the system continuously. That points to a leak somewhere in the drainage system or the lung. The nurse also checks vital signs for hypotension or tachycardia from blood loss, breath sounds for atelectasis, and the suture line for infection. (study guide, Ch 26)",
 "when palliative care can begin": "Palliative care is built around the client's symptoms and quality of life, not around how long the client is expected to live. Lung cancer causes pain, dyspnea and nausea from the time it is diagnosed, so the help is needed from day one. For that reason palliative care starts at diagnosis and continues alongside chemotherapy or radiation. It is not the same as hospice, which is for the end of life. (study guide, Ch 26 Connection Check)",
 "CRAB criteria used to support a diagnosis": "Each letter of CRAB comes from a way the abnormal plasma cells damage the body. Lytic bone lesions release calcium, which causes hypercalcemia and also bone disease with fractures. Excess light chains injure the kidney, which causes renal insufficiency, and the crowded marrow causes anemia. Plasma cells of 10% or more in the marrow and an M protein in serum or urine complete the picture. (study guide, Ch 34)",
 "incurable B-cell cancer of plasma cells": "A single clone of plasma cells, which are mature B cells that make antibodies, overgrows the marrow and cannot be fully removed. Because the clone is spread through the marrow and bones, treatment aims to control the disease and its complications rather than cure it. That is why the nurse focuses on pain control, calcium and kidney protection, infection and fracture prevention. Chemotherapy, dexamethasone, bisphosphonates and an autologous stem cell transplant are options. (study guide, Ch 34)",
 "thalidomide or lenalidomide for multiple myeloma that these drugs are safe": "These two drugs interfere with the normal development of a fetus, so they are called teratogenic. A woman who becomes pregnant while taking them could have a baby with serious defects. The safe teaching is that a woman of childbearing age needs reliable contraception, and the same two-forms-of-birth-control rule appears for methotrexate in the class case. Claiming they are safe in pregnancy is therefore wrong. (study guide, Ch 34; class notes)",
 "daily stool softener": "After prostate surgery there are fresh wounds and vessels in and around the bladder neck. Straining to pass a hard stool raises pressure in the pelvis and can restart bleeding or dislodge clots, so the nurse prevents it. A stool softener keeps the stool soft so the client does not strain. It works together with bladder irrigation, which clears clots, and aseptic wound care. (study guide, Ch 66)",
 "radioactive implant for prostate cancer": "Brachytherapy places the radiation source inside or next to the tumor, so the client can expose others to radiation. The nurse therefore minimizes contact and wears proper, thicker PPE. This differs from external beam radiation, where the machine delivers the dose and the client is not radioactive. Body fluids of a brachytherapy client may carry radioactive material, so the family is taught the precautions too. (study guide, Ch 13, Ch 66 and class notes)",
 "fever, malaise and a rising white blood cell count": "Cancer and its treatment weaken the body's defenses, and surgery or catheters add entry points for bacteria. Fever, malaise and a rising WBC are the body's response to an infection, so infection is the most likely cause and is checked first. After prostate surgery infection also shows as increasing pain, swelling, redness, drainage and nausea or vomiting. The nurse reports these findings and uses aseptic wound care. (study guide, Ch 66 and class PLQ)",
 "African American men have a higher incidence of prostate cancer": "The risk of prostate cancer is not the same in every group. African American men, and Caribbean men of African ancestry, have a higher incidence and about twice the mortality of other men. Because of this the screening conversation starts earlier, at age 45 rather than 50. The nurse should know the disparity when discussing PSA and rectal exam screening. (study guide, Ch 66 and Ch 13)",
 "benign prostatic hyperplasia is an early stage": "BPH is an enlargement of the gland from changes such as dihydrotestosterone buildup, and it squeezes the urethra. It is a benign process and does not turn into cancer. A man can have both conditions at the same time, which is why PSA cannot separate them and why a biopsy is used to confirm cancer. The statement that BPH is an early stage of cancer is therefore false. (study guide, Ch 66)",
 "risk factors from the cancer risk table": "Each cancer has exposures that damage its own organ, and the table pairs them. H. pylori inflames the stomach lining, alcohol and viral hepatitis injure the liver, and an undescended testicle is a developmental problem of the testis. Asbestos and tobacco damage the airway lining. Knowing which exposure hits which organ lets the nurse match a client's history to the cancer. (study guide, Ch 13 Table 13.2)",
 "BRCA2 mutation asks which cancers": "BRCA genes normally repair DNA, so a mutation lets damaged cells survive and multiply. BRCA1 raises the risk of breast and ovarian cancer. BRCA2 raises those too and adds pancreatic and prostate cancer and melanoma, and it is the more common mutation in male breast cancer. The other options belong to other exposures such as tobacco, asbestos or HPV. (study guide, Ch 13 and Ch 66)",
 "red flags for hereditary cancer": "A hereditary syndrome is a mutation that is present in every cell from birth, so it shows up as cancer that appears earlier, more often, or in unusual places. That explains the red flags: multiple primaries, bilateral tumors in paired organs, a younger-than-usual age, a tumor in the sex not usually affected, and two or more first-degree relatives with the same cancer. A single tumor at the usual age with no family history does not fit the pattern. These clients are referred for genetic counseling. (study guide, Ch 13)",
 "The A stands for a [1]": "CAUTION is a memory aid for the changes that early cancers cause in tissue. Each letter names a change: bowel or bladder habits, a sore that does not heal, unusual bleeding or discharge, a thickening or lump, indigestion or difficulty swallowing, an obvious change in a wart or mole, and a nagging cough or hoarseness. Benign causes usually settle in days, so a sign that lasts more than 2 weeks needs evaluation. (study guide, Ch 13)",
 "carcinogen exposure is the most common risk factor for cancer": "A carcinogen damages DNA, and cancer develops when damage builds up faster than the cell and immune system can repair or remove it. For that reason one exposure rarely causes cancer; multiple exposures plus genetic mutations that the immune system misses are what lead to it. The risks are cumulative over a lifetime. Carcinogen exposure is therefore the most common risk factor. (study guide, Ch 13)",
 "most cancers come from known genetic syndromes": "Inherited mutations such as BRCA1/2, p53 and FAP are present from birth and raise the risk sharply, but they are uncommon. They account for only 5 to 10% of cancers. Most cancers come from exposures and age, which add damage over time. The statement that most cancers come from known syndromes is therefore false. (study guide, Ch 13)",
 "serum calcium of 12 mg/dL, confusion and polyuria": "In lymphoma, bone involvement releases calcium into the blood, and high calcium makes the kidneys lose water. That causes polyuria and dehydration, and the high level affects the brain and the heart, causing confusion and heart block. A calcium above 11 mg/dL is an emergency. IV fluids replace the losses and dilute the calcium, so they are the expected intervention. (study guide, Ch 34)",
 "involvement of two lymph node regions on the same side": "Lymphoma staging follows how far the disease has traveled through lymph tissue. Stage I is a single node, stage II is two or more nodes on the same side of the diaphragm, and stage III is nodes on both sides. Stage IV means organs are involved as well as nodes. The letters E for extranodal and S for spleen add detail. (study guide, Ch 34)",
 "CBC, chest x-ray and CT scan. Which test is definitive": "Blood tests and imaging show that something is wrong and how far it has spread, but they cannot show what the cells are. Uric acid and LDH rise from high cell turnover, and the CBC can show marrow involvement. Only tissue can prove lymphoma, so a lymph node biopsy is definitive. A bone marrow biopsy or lumbar puncture is added if marrow or CNS involvement is suspected. (study guide, Ch 34)",
 "dry cough that is worse lying flat may mean a mediastinal mass": "Lymph nodes in the chest can enlarge into a mediastinal mass. When the client lies flat the mass presses on the trachea and irritates it, which causes a dry persistent cough that worsens when lying down. Because the airway can be compressed, this sign is a warning of an airway risk and needs prompt evaluation. The nurse should not dismiss the cough. (study guide, Ch 34)",
 "generalized itching with scratch marks is more common in non-Hodgkin": "Hodgkin lymphoma often causes generalized itching, and scratching leaves excoriation. The skin changes are one of the recognizable features that help separate it from non-Hodgkin lymphoma. Both lymphomas cause painless node swelling and B symptoms. The true teaching is that itching is more typical of Hodgkin lymphoma. (study guide, Ch 34)",
 "told her breast cancer is stage III": "Staging puts the extent of disease on a scale from small and local to spread. Stage I is small with no obvious spread outside the organ, stage II invades deeper tissue or local nodes, stage III is large or locally advanced and possibly in local nodes, and stage IV has metastasized to other organs. Your class notes recorded a different answer for this case (spread to the lymph and blood systems), but the textbook definition is the one the case answer key supports, so confirm with your instructor. (study guide, Ch 13 and class PLQ)",
 "Match each term with its meaning": "Staging and grading answer two different questions. Staging asks how big the tumor is and how far it has spread, using T for size, N for the number of lymph nodes and M for metastasis. Grading asks how differentiated the cells look under the microscope. Poorly differentiated cells no longer look like the parent cell, so they are harder to treat and more aggressive. (study guide, Ch 13)",
 "prostate cancer is described as T2": "In the prostate system the T number shows how far the tumor has grown locally. T1 cannot be felt on exam, T2 is confined to the prostate, T3 is locally extensive, and T4 invades neighboring organs. A Gleason grade describes the cells separately: two sites are graded 1 to 5 and added, for a maximum of 10. A higher score means more poorly differentiated cells. (study guide, Ch 66)",
 "Gleason score of 9": "Two sites in the biopsy are each graded from 1 to 5 and the grades are added. Grade 1 is well differentiated, looks nearly normal, and has the best prognosis. Grade 5 is poorly differentiated and has a poor prognosis. A total of 9 out of a maximum of 10 therefore means poorly differentiated cells and a worse outlook. The score is a grade, not a stage or a cure percentage. (study guide, Ch 66)",
 "ovarian cancer has spread to the peritoneum": "Ovarian cancer is staged from what the surgeon finds, because it is not biopsied beforehand. The FIGO stages follow how far the disease has spread from the ovaries: stage I is limited to the ovaries and stage II extends into the pelvis. Stage III means the peritoneum and/or lymph nodes are involved, and stage IV is distant spread. Spread to the peritoneum and nodes is therefore stage III. (study guide, Ch 65)",
 "all hematologic malignancies are considered stage IV": "Solid tumors start in an organ and spread outward, so size and spread give a stage. Leukemia, lymphoma and multiple myeloma start in blood-forming or lymph tissue, which circulates through the whole body. They therefore involve the body from the start and are classed as stage IV at diagnosis. The statement is accurate. (study guide, Ch 13)",
 "grade of a tumor describes how far the cancer has spread": "Grade and stage measure different things. Grade describes how differentiated the cells look under the microscope, and poorly differentiated cells are more aggressive. Stage describes size, lymph node involvement and metastasis, which is how far the cancer has spread. The statement mixes the two up and is false. (study guide, Ch 13)",
 "screening options for a healthy 40-year-old client": "Cervical cancer develops slowly through dysplasia, and the HPV test finds the virus that causes it earlier than the Pap finds cell changes. Because of that, from age 30 to 65 the interval can be longer when HPV testing is included: primary HPV or a Pap and HPV cotest every 5 years. A Pap alone is repeated every 3 years. Clients with HIV, immunosuppression, DES exposure or prior dysplasia may need screening more often. (study guide, Ch 65)",
 "67-year-old client has had adequate negative cervical screenings": "Cervical cancer takes years to develop from dysplasia, so a long record of normal results makes new disease unlikely. After age 65, screening may stop if prior screening was adequate and negative and there has been no CIN 2 in 20 years. A hysterectomy for a benign cause, with no history of CIN 2, also ends screening. Screening continues only when risk is higher. (study guide, Ch 65)",
 "scheduled for a colposcopy. Which statement": "Colposcopy magnifies the cervix after an abnormal Pap, and acetic acid or Lugol's solution is applied so abnormal tissue stands out. The client is told to put nothing in the vagina for 24 hours beforehand. The client empties the bladder, and the test is rescheduled during heavy menstruation. Lugol's contains iodine, so it is contraindicated with an iodine or shellfish allergy. (study guide, Ch 64)",
 "AFP and hCG levels stayed elevated after orchiectomy": "Alpha-fetoprotein and hCG are made by testicular tumor cells and enter the blood. Once the tumor is removed the levels should fall. If they stay high, tumor tissue that makes them must still be present somewhere, perhaps in lymph nodes or other organs. The nurse reports persistent elevation, because it means more tumor should be looked for. Staging uses TNM plus S for the serum markers. (study guide, Ch 66)",
 "testicular cancer facts. Match each item": "Seminoma and nonseminoma behave differently, which decides treatment. Seminoma is slow growing and very sensitive to radiation, so surgery is followed by radiation and/or chemotherapy. Nonseminoma grows and spreads faster and does not respond to radiation, so it gets surgery, node removal and chemotherapy. The chemotherapy drugs also have typical toxicities: bleomycin can injure the lungs and ifosfamide can cause hemorrhagic cystitis. (study guide, Ch 66)",
 "returns from an orchiectomy with a blood pressure of 88/50": "After surgery the body loses blood and fluid from the surgical site, and the heart beats faster to keep the output up. When the volume lost is significant the blood pressure falls, so low blood pressure with a fast heart rate means volume loss. Infection would show as fever instead. The nurse reports the vital signs and watches the wound for bleeding. (study guide, Ch 66)",
 "alpha-fetoprotein and hCG levels should fall after the tumor is removed": "The tumor cells are the source of AFP and hCG, so when the tumor is removed the markers should drop. A fall confirms that the source was removed. A level that does not fall suggests tumor tissue elsewhere. The nurse therefore follows the markers as a measure of treatment success. (study guide, Ch 66)",
 "what a liquid biopsy is": "A liquid biopsy is a blood test that looks for cancer cells or tumor DNA, so it does not take tissue from the mass. It is used for example in non-small cell lung, breast, colorectal, GI and ovarian cancers. It differs from tissue biopsies, which remove cells or a mass directly. (study guide, Ch 13)",
 "results of a tissue biopsy usually take 3 to 10 days": "A biopsy sample has to be processed, stained and examined by a pathologist before a cytopathologic diagnosis can be made, and that takes time. Results are expected in 3 to 10 days. The nurse explains the purpose, preparation, aftercare and when results arrive, so the client is not left waiting without information. The statement is accurate. (study guide, Ch 13)",
 "A 60-year-old postmenopausal client calls the clinic": "After menopause the uterine lining should not bleed, so bleeding means the lining is abnormal. Uterine cancer is most common at ages 55 to 65, with a median of 60, and abnormal bleeding is its most common symptom. Any postmenopausal bleeding needs evaluation, so this statement is the one that needs immediate follow-up. The other statements are not the key warning sign for a gynecologic cancer. (study guide, Ch 65 and class PLQ)",
 "risk factors for uterine (endometrial) cancer": "Estrogen makes the uterine lining grow, and progesterone balances it. When estrogen is unopposed, by estrogen therapy without progesterone, tamoxifen or anovulation, the lining keeps growing and can become cancer. Obesity adds risk because fat tissue makes estrogen. Chapter 13 also lists early menarche, late menopause, PCOS and diabetes. (study guide, Ch 65 and Ch 13)",
 "most common type of uterine cancer": "Most uterine cancers arise from the lining, the endometrium. About 80% are endometrioid adenocarcinoma, called type I, which is driven by estrogen and is usually found early with about 88% survival. Type II (serous or clear cell) is aggressive and occurs in older clients with a poor prognosis, and sarcoma of the muscle is only 3 to 5%. The most common type is therefore the estrogen-driven endometrioid type. (study guide, Ch 65)",
 "Which diagnostic test does the nurse expect to be done first for uterine cancer": "Bleeding comes from the endometrium, so the test samples the endometrium. The endometrial biopsy is 99% sensitive for high-grade cancer and is done first. A pelvic transvaginal ultrasound shows a thickened endometrium, and a D&C is added if the biopsy is negative but bleeding continues. CA-125, colposcopy and mammography belong to ovarian, cervical and breast evaluation. (study guide, Ch 65)",
 "endometrial biopsy is negative but she continues": "A biopsy takes a small sample and can miss an area of disease, so a negative result does not explain ongoing bleeding. A D&C scrapes the whole lining and gives a larger tissue sample for pathology. It is done with NPO status and consent, and normal postoperative bleeding and cramping are expected. Continued bleeding always needs further evaluation. (study guide, Ch 65 and Ch 64)",
 "stomatitis. Which instruction": "Chemotherapy kills fast-dividing cells, including the lining of the mouth, so sores develop. Hot or spicy food irritates the damaged lining and worsens pain. Salt-water rinses keep the mouth clean and comfortable, and antiemetics help with nausea. Soft toothbrushes and good hydration also help. (study guide, Ch 65 and Ch 13)",
 "anyone who takes estrogen therapy and has a uterus also needs progesterone": "Estrogen makes the lining of the uterus grow, and progesterone keeps that growth in check. Estrogen alone is unopposed, and years of unopposed growth raise the risk of uterine cancer. A client who has a uterus therefore takes progesterone with estrogen. The teaching is accurate. (study guide, Ch 65)",
 "ovarian cancer is the most common gynecologic cancer in the United States": "Two different facts are easy to swap. Uterine (endometrial) cancer is the most common gynecologic cancer in the US, with a 2.6% lifetime risk. Ovarian cancer is the deadliest, because its symptoms are vague and 70 to 75% are not found until stage III or IV. The statement gives the label of the wrong cancer and is false. (study guide, Ch 65)",
 "teaching a client with neutropenia about precautions at home": "Neutrophils are the first cells to reach an infection, so with a low count even ordinary germs can cause serious infection. Each precaution removes a source of germs: other people's hands and coughs, live plants and flowers that carry bacteria and mold, standing water, fruit that is hard to wash, and the rectal route. The client washes produce, avoids raspberries and blackberries, and uses no rectal temperatures, suppositories or enemas. Your class notes add reverse isolation, where staff wear PPE to protect the client. (study guide, Ch 34 Box 34.2 and class notes)",
 "A client with neutropenia is being discharged": "Live plants and cut flowers carry bacteria and mold, and a neutropenic client cannot fight them off. For that reason they are avoided at home and in the hospital, and a statement about placing flowers at the bedside shows a need for teaching. The correct statements are washing raw fruits and vegetables, checking the temperature daily and calling for more than 100.4°F (38°C), and taking prophylactic antimicrobials daily and finishing the course. These actions lower the chance of infection or catch it early. (study guide, Ch 34 Box 34.2)",
 "temperature of 100.6°F": "In a chemotherapy client, few working neutrophils means the usual signs of infection may be weak or missing, and a fever may be the only sign. An infection can spread to the blood and lead to sepsis within hours. Treatment with IV antibiotics prevents that, so the client should seek care or call the provider right away. Waiting to see whether the fever settles delays treatment. (study guide, Ch 13 and Ch 34)",
 "ANC of 450/mm³. Which order": "Rectal tissue is full of bacteria, and a suppository, enema or thermometer can create tiny tears that let them into the blood. With an ANC of 450 the client cannot control that infection. For that reason rectal medications and temperatures are not used in neutropenia. Prophylactic antibiotics, a private room and oral temperatures are appropriate. (study guide, Ch 34 Box 34.2)",
 "Which visitor should the nurse restrict": "A neutropenic client has few cells to fight germs, so any visitor with a communicable illness is a direct risk. A sibling with a cold and a cough could pass on the infection. Well visitors who clean their hands are allowed. The plan also avoids crowds, small children and pets, and requires hand hygiene by everyone. (study guide, Ch 34 and class PLQ)",
 "neutropenia values with a new graduate": "The ANC counts the neutrophils that can fight bacteria and fungi. Below 1,500 the client is neutropenic and at risk of infection, and at 500 or below the risk is high enough that neutropenic precautions are started. A temperature of 100.4°F (38°C) or higher in a chemotherapy client is called in immediately and treated with broad-spectrum IV antibiotics. Matching each number with its action is the core of the care plan. (study guide, Ch 13 and Ch 34)",
 "reverse isolation for a neutropenic client": "In ordinary isolation the barrier protects other people from the client's germs. In reverse isolation the direction is reversed, because the neutropenic client is the one who cannot fight germs. Staff and visitors wear PPE when required, limit their exposures, and avoid items that harbor waterborne organisms. The nurse explaining that the PPE protects the client is accurate. (class notes and study guide, Ch 34)",
 "raspberries and blackberries are safe to eat because they are easy to wash": "Fruits and vegetables carry soil bacteria on their surface, and a neutropenic client cannot fight them off. Raspberries and blackberries have a bumpy surface that traps dirt and is hard to clean. For that reason they are avoided, while other raw fruits and vegetables are washed well. The statement is false. (study guide, Ch 34 Box 34.2)",
}
